{"challenges":[{"attack":"A rational representation might have a negative denominator, so minimality over positive denominators could be unavailable.","claim_id":"claim-integer-representation","outcome":"survives","result":"step-positive-denominator changes p/q to -p/-q when q<0, yielding b>0. Thus the set used later is nonempty.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"The least-denominator choice could improperly assume that every nonempty set of positive integers has a least member.","claim_id":"claim-minimal-denominator","outcome":"survives","result":"step-minimal-denominator explicitly applies the well-ordering principle to the nonempty set of positive denominators. This is a valid application of the named standard result.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Squaring or clearing denominators might introduce an invalid division-by-zero step.","claim_id":"claim-square-equation","outcome":"survives","result":"The chosen denominator satisfies b>0, hence b² is nonzero. Squaring and multiplying by b² are valid.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"From a² even, it does not generally follow without proof that a is even.","claim_id":"claim-a-even","outcome":"survives","result":"step-a-even supplies the needed contrapositive calculation: an odd integer 2k+1 has odd square.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"After substituting a=2k, cancellation by 2 might be unjustified.","claim_id":"claim-b-square-even","outcome":"survives","result":"The equality 4k²=2b² is an integer equality; dividing both sides by the nonzero integer 2 yields b²=2k².","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"The same parity inference for b could be an unproved repetition.","claim_id":"claim-b-even","outcome":"survives","result":"step-b-even again gives the explicit odd-square calculation and contradicts the established evenness of b².","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Halving a and b might yield a zero or nonintegral denominator, or fail to be smaller.","claim_id":"claim-irrational","outcome":"survives","result":"With a=2k and b=2l, k,l are integers. Since b=2l>0, l>0, and l=b/2<b. Thus step-halved-representation supplies a forbidden smaller positive denominator.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"The contradiction may only refute the selected representation rather than the original rationality assumption.","claim_id":"claim-irrational","outcome":"survives","result":"The selected representation was obtained from the temporary rationality assumption, and the contradiction is with its least-denominator property. Therefore the temporary assumption is discharged, yielding irrationality.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"This is a scoped contradiction assumption, and step-contradiction depends on steps in its declared scope and closes the subproof.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-positive-denominator","step-minimal-denominator","step-square-equation","step-a-even","step-b-square-even","step-b-even","step-halved-representation","step-contradiction"],"claim_id":"claim-rational-assumption","reasoning":"The assumption is not asserted as true; it is legitimately introduced for contradiction and discharged by step-contradiction.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"Under the temporary rationality assumption, the definition of rational supplies an integer quotient with nonzero denominator; step-positive-denominator makes that denominator positive.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-positive-denominator"],"claim_id":"claim-integer-representation","reasoning":"The representation and sign normalization are valid and remain within the temporary assumption's scope.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The well-ordering principle is named, and step-minimal-denominator explicitly applies it to the nonempty set established in step-positive-denominator.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-positive-denominator","step-minimal-denominator"],"claim_id":"claim-minimal-denominator","reasoning":"Every member of the specified set is a positive integer, so its least member gives the required selected representation.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"step-square-equation follows by valid algebra from the selected equality, with b>0 ensuring b² is nonzero.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-minimal-denominator","step-square-equation"],"claim_id":"claim-square-equation","reasoning":"Squaring √2=a/b gives 2=a²/b², and multiplying by b² yields a²=2b².","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"step-a-even proves the contrapositive needed for the parity inference using the displayed odd-square calculation.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-square-equation","step-a-even"],"claim_id":"claim-a-even","reasoning":"Since a²=2b² is even, a cannot be odd; hence a is even.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"Substitution in step-b-square-even directly derives b²=2k² from the equation and the even representation of a.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-square-equation","step-a-even","step-b-square-even"],"claim_id":"claim-b-square-even","reasoning":"The displayed calculation establishes that b² is even.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"step-b-even supplies the same explicit contrapositive parity argument for b.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-b-square-even","step-b-even"],"claim_id":"claim-b-even","reasoning":"An odd b would have an odd square, contradicting step-b-square-even.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"step-contradiction depends on the scoped rationality subproof through the least-denominator choice and the smaller positive denominator constructed in step-halved-representation.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-minimal-denominator","step-a-even","step-b-even","step-halved-representation","step-contradiction"],"claim_id":"claim-irrational","reasoning":"The smaller denominator l is positive and strictly below b, contradicting minimality. This discharges the temporary rationality assumption and proves √2 irrational.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
